A||||13||FieroLisa||DOT 3 vs. DOT 5||||1||
Z||000000||FieroLisa||07-29-2001||04:56 PM||||Ok guys, heres my question of the day. I know dot 3 and dot 5 cannot be mixed for brake fluid. but WHAT happens when it IS mixed? The reason I ask is because when I bought my car, It had a little post it note that said "Use DOT 5 only" which I guess is feasible that he switched to synthetic. <P>But then I remember when I lowered my car, I bled the brakes and I know I didn't buy anything expensive, and Brian said the only thing he has is dot 3, which means that's probably what went in there.<P>Now my brakes are crappy, I can't feel any leaks, but the fluid is way low. I want to fill it, but don't know what to use!!!!! ARG! Worst case is I flush out the brakes on Wednesday and start from scratch but I can't drive the car till then in that case.<P>The guy at Advanced Auto was a total idiot. I asked him the question about mixing the fluids and he jusy stared at me stupidly. When he didn't have the answer he said "You gotta take them there cars to a specialist cause they a little different" I was like "Um it's got a master cylinder, brake lines and calipers, OK? It's just a brake system"  He kinda sulked off.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||07-29-2001||05:44 PM||||I don't think you messed anything up, but I'm pretty sure you're gonna have to flush the system if you've mixed fluids.  Orief or The Ogre have some info on this.  <P>Let me and Pat know if you need some help getting that done before Williamsburg.  Can't miss that just because your brakes need bleeding!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||07-29-2001||05:59 PM||||From what i've read on the internet somewhere....(you're right)you aren't supposed to mix the DOT5 with anything.  if you do, any problems will be because of the mix.  Any little air bubbles in the system will make big air bubbles.  So you have to bleed the crap out of it.  Most people say that the DOT 5 will eat through rubber lines and stuff...dunno.  Most show car(your car is definetly worthy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) people use DOT 5 because it doesn't eat paint...but I would flush the system and go with the old stuff.<P>Joe<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 07-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||starfighter007@msn.com||07-29-2001||07:26 PM||||Well I had a jaguar and I put dot 3 in when it should have taken dot5.the little bit of dot 3 ate up all the rubber parts in the brake system.What a costly mistake that was .I was told you can add dot 5 to dot 3 systems but not dot 3 to dot 5 .Reason that I was told that the jag. rubber parts in the brake system were made of natural rubber.And the dot 5 is not corrsive,like dot 3 brake fluid.If it were me in your place I think I would drain and flush the brake system and go with silicone brake fluid period.I am due for a brake overhaul pretty soon and I am going to convert to silicone brake fluid.hope this info was useful.||||reg||1
Z||000004||kslish||07-29-2001||08:30 PM||||What color is the brake fluid?  The reason I ask is nearly all DOT 5 fluids are dyed a blueish purple color as sort of a warning so you know it's different from the norm.  If its purple, it's DOT 5 Silicone fluid.  If not, you are 99.9% safe in adding normal fluid (unless there is some really old non color coded silicone in there, but I doubt it).<P>Now some basics off the top of my head...<P>DOT 5 brake fluid is silicone based.  DOT 3 (standard) and DOT 4 (heavy-duty) are glycol-based.  DOT 5 silicone brake fluid does not absorb moisture over time as conventional DOT 3 and 4 brake fluids do (so it doesn't go bad with age or exposure to air).  Silicone is also nontoxic and won't damage paint like conventional brake fluid.  It also has a higher boiling point (which makes it popular in some forms of racing).<P>Because of these reasons, it is often marketed as a premium "lifetime" brake fluid.  It is often used to preserve brake systems in antique and classic vehicles and those that sit for long periods of time between use.<P>Silicone also has slightly different physical properties and compressibility, due to this NEVER use DOT 5 in a car with ABS brakes....ABS systems are calibrated to work with DOT 3 or 4 brake types of fluid only and silicone won't lubricate the ABS pump properly like normal DOT 3-4 fluid.<P>Also, DOT 5 Silicone won't mix with glyco-based (DOT 3 and 4) brake fluid and CAN create sludge if mixed (NOT GOOD).  Basically the two fluids mix about as good as oil and water.  Mixing the two can create a gelatin like goo that will eventually plug up stuff or cause the caliper pistons to stick. <P>Here is a good link to check out about the differences between DOT 3-4 and 5:  <A HREF="http://www.xs11.com/tips/maintenance/maint1.shtml" TARGET=_blank>http://www.xs11.com/tips/maintenance/maint1.shtml</A> <P>I'd personally never put DOT 5 silicone in any of my cars.... the fact it does not abosrb water is not necessarily a good thing on a street driven car (since if moisture does get into the system, instead of mixing with the brake fluid so it can be flushed out easily, the moisture gets trapped at low points in the system and causes rust and/or corrosion) along with various other disadvantages (like causing spongy pedal feel since it compresses more) unless the brake system was designed with silicone in mind (like maybe starfighter007's Jaguar).  I'm sure theogre will back me up on this if he's around.  <P>Okay off my soapbox now....<P>Hopefully Lisa, you have regular brake fluid in your car.  If so, you probably either have contaminated fluid (meaning it's been exposed to too much moisture) or a small leak somewhere causing the lousy brake feel.  If you can jack the car up or put it on a lift, check all the steel lines for fluid leaks.  Particularly in the front where the lines come through the spare tire well and make sharp turns to each side of the car (one of my Fieros got a pinhole leak from rust there) and towards the back near the distribution block...this is another common place for lines to go bad.  If no line leak is found and no calipers are leaking, I'd recommend a flush with new fluid.  Valvoline SynPower synthetic DOT 4 is simply the sh!t when it comes to normal brake fluid.  Plus it is available for like $5 a quart at your local Wal-Mart.  Here is a link so you know what the bottle looks like: <A HREF="http://www.valvoline.com/synpower/synproducts/enginefluids/index.asp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.valvoline.com/synpower/synproducts/enginefluids/index.asp</A> <P>I couldn't find another economical DOT 3-4 compatible fluid on the market that could hold a candle to specs of the Valvoline synthetic, so it's what I ended up putting in my car.  MUCH better pedal feel than the old fluid that was in there before the lines went bad.<P>Before someone asks why do I know all this crap?  It's because I researched it when I had all the steel brake lines replaced in my '86 Fiero.  Since I had to refill the entire system I wanted the best fluid for the job.  That and I have too much time on my hands            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A><BR><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 07-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroLisa||07-29-2001||08:52 PM||||wow Ken, I'm glad I have such a knowlegdeable "fiancee"  <P>Thanks and I'm just waiting till Wednesday to flush the fluid and refill, although not sure if I'm going to use 3, 4 or 5....depends on how cheap I'm being that day||||reg||1
Z||000006||kslish||07-29-2001||09:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroLisa:<BR><B>I'm going to use 3, 4 or 5....depends on how cheap I'm being that day</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lisa, Lisa, Lisa.....if you don't have DOT 5 already in your system, spend the 5 darn bucks on a quart of the Valvoline synthetic fluid.  It's like $2 more than the cheap stuff.  I even have a quart I could give you if you weren't like 500 miles away!  Ya need to arrive at these Fiero shows intact if you are ever going to get that ring....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Ken<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||beachbomb||07-29-2001||09:20 PM||||Hey Lisa, sounds like the car is a total loss to me, if you want to sell it, i'll take it!!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">..i don't have a clue about this question, good luck!!..JIm(2 '84 Indys)||||reg||10
Z||000008||Formula88||07-29-2001||11:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroLisa:<BR><B>wow Ken, I'm glad I have such a knowlegdeable "fiancee"  <P>Thanks and I'm just waiting till Wednesday to flush the fluid and refill, although not sure if I'm going to use 3, 4 or 5....depends on how cheap I'm being that day</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lisa, I woulnd't recommend going with DOT 5 if you're flushing your system.  It came with DOT 4, I'd go back with that, or the Valvoline SynPower synthetic DOT 4 <B>if</B> it says it's DOT 3 compatible on the bottle.  If so, that seems like the best way to go.  DOT 5 really needs to only be in systems that were designed for it, or seldom driven show cars.  <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000009||FieroLisa||07-30-2001||10:16 AM||||The car sat for 6 years before I bought it, the owner waiting for it to go up in value.... So I guess I could see why he might put dot 5 in.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||07-30-2001||01:28 PM||||I just looked at a can of severe duty brake fluid from Wagner and it's labeled DOT 5.1 and the can says that this stuff is DOT 3 and 4 compatible. This stuff is glycol based. Anybody know anything this?||||reg||1
Z||000011||kslish||07-30-2001||02:18 PM||||Formula88, Valvoline SynPower DOT 4 fluid is completely backwards compatible with DOT 3.  <P>Phil, DOT 5.1 is a fairly new designation for what is essentially a new-tech "Super DOT 4" fluid.  It is 100% synthetic, but like you said is glycol based so it is backwards compatible also with DOT 3 & 4.  Why the DOT made a designation called 5.1 is completely beyond me when DOT 5 silicone is a completely different animal entirely.  I guess it's called 5.1 because it exceeds the minimal boiling point specs for DOT 5 silicone.  DOT 5.1 is also just as incompatible with DOT 5 silicone as DOT 3 & 4 are.<P>DOT 5.1 also lubricates a little better than DOT 3 & 4 and is less corrosive when it gets old so some prefer it in cars with expensive ABS braking systems. <P>DOT 5.1 tends to be expensive though for what benefit that you do get out of it, which is why I still recommend the Valvoline synthetic DOT 4 stuff which is much more reasonably priced and is a much better fluid than standard DOT 3 or 4 for a minimal price increase. <P>Ken S.<P>P.S. - Lisa, have you checked the color of the fluid in the brake reservoir yet?  Is it blue-purple (DOT 5) or some shade of amber (DOT 3-4)?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 07-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||07-30-2001||07:04 PM||||The good news is, hopefully, you didn't mess up the system.<P>The bad news is all the DOT 5 Silicone MUST come out and a simple flush will not do it.<P>Flushing simply can't get the old fluid. The fronts will trap a little bit but the OE back ones will trap a bunch, like around 4 ounces, that will refuse to be flushed. The silicone will continue to cause problems untill it is removed.<P>To get out of this cheaply. buy rebuild kits for all 4 wheels. Flush the MC and lines clear of the contaminated fluid then rebuild the calipers. This is the only way you'll get all the silicone out.<P>Use the Valvoline Synthetic. It is the best fluid for the money that I've seen.<P>there is data and a bunch of links on this in my cave.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroLisa||07-30-2001||08:03 PM||||The fluid is definitely an amber color: Does that mean its 100% surely 3 or 4? Is there any way dot 5 would be amber color?<P>||||reg||1
